---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import SvgOptimizer from '../../components/SvgOptimizer.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="SVG Optimizer (Basic)" description="Strip comments, whitespace, and unnecessary IDs from an SVG to reduce its size.">
  <SvgOptimizer client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why exported SVGs are often much larger than they need to be</h2>
  <p>SVGs exported from design tools frequently include editor metadata, unused IDs, and generous whitespace that add file size without any visual effect — a basic cleanup pass strips these safely, and dedicated tools can go further by simplifying the actual path geometry.</p>

  <h2>How to use this tool</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste your SVG markup.</li>
    <li>Copy the cleaned-up output, with the size reduction shown.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common SVG Optimization Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Expecting full optimization</strong> like a dedicated tool (SVGO) would provide — this handles basic whitespace/comment/ID cleanup, not path simplification or advanced geometry optimization.</li>
    <li><strong>Removing IDs that are actually referenced</strong> by CSS or JavaScript elsewhere — check for external references before assuming all IDs are safe to strip.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This SVG Optimizer</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly trimming an SVG exported from a design tool before using it on a website</li>
    <li>Reducing icon file sizes for a small performance improvement</li>
    <li>Cleaning up SVG markup before embedding it inline in HTML</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>SVG Optimizer FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Should I use a dedicated tool like SVGO instead?</strong> For production assets where every byte matters, yes — SVGO and similar tools can also simplify path geometry and merge shapes, going further than this basic cleanup.</p>
</ToolLayout>
